 ession Description\n\nThe second Trump administration has undertaken a ser
 ies of decisions with profound and lasting consequences for the health and
  survival of Africa’s children. The abrupt dismantling of the United Sta
 tes Agency for International Development (USAID) is projected to result in
  approximately 14 million excess deaths\, the majority concentrated in sub
 -Saharan Africa. Concurrently\, the decision to initiate hostilities with 
 Iran and the ensuing disruption of traffic through the Strait of Hormuz ha
 ve severely constrained global fertilizer exports to African markets. Mode
 ling informed by NASA agricultural forecasts indicates that the 2027 growi
 ng season in the Sahel is at high risk of significant production shortfall
 s and elevated famine risk. This talk presents ongoing research from the S
 tanford Geopolitical Human Impact Research Lab on the cascading effects of
  geopolitical decisions on human health. It focuses specifically on the pr
 ojected impacts of the Strait of Hormuz crisis on food security and child 
 nutrition across the Sahel in the 2027 season and outlines concrete policy
  options through which the United States can still act to avert a larger-s
 cale humanitarian catastrophe.\n\nSpeaker Bio\n\nDr. Ruth Gibson is the Fo
 unding Executive Director of the Stanford Geopolitical Human Impact Resear
 ch Lab\, the first research lab at Stanford University dedicated to examin
 ing how geopolitical decisions shape human lives\, with particular attenti
